{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"friendless-alabaster-cub","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Class\n\n`src/rebar/_store/lock.py` — **384 `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'flock'`\nplus 4 `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'fcntl'`** in job `100874330553`, roughly 74% of the\n510 failures and the single largest class by an order of magnitude.\n\n`lock.py:72-75` guards the **import**:\n\n```python\ntry:  # POSIX advisory locking; absent on some platforms (e.g. plain Windows)\n    import fcntl\nexcept ImportError:\n    fcntl = None\n```\n\nbut there is no `is not None` check at either **use** site — `lock.py:207` in `_acquire_fcntl` and\n`lock.py:254` in `write_lock_is_busy`. Every store write reaches `_acquire_once` → `_acquire_fcntl`\n(`lock.py:491`), so on Windows every ticket write raises.\n\nSibling modules already guard correctly (`_snapshot/git_fetch.py:79`, `_snapshot/janitor.py:430`,\n`reducer/marker.py:33`), which makes `lock.py` an **outlier rather than a deliberate choice**.\n\n## THE OBVIOUS TWO-LINE FIX IS UNSAFE. DO NOT TAKE IT.\n\nSkipping the fcntl leg when `fcntl is None` looks like two lines. It silently breaks a documented,\nload-bearing invariant.\n\n`_acquire_mkdir` (`lock.py:325-362`) passes `fcntl_held=True` into stale-lock reclamation, and its\nown docstring states the precondition verbatim:\n\n> **Precondition (load bearing, hence `fcntl_held=True` below):** this is only ever reached from\n> `acquire` AFTER the exclusive `fcntl.flock(LOCK_EX)` leg on the same tracker's\n> `.ticket-write.lock` has been taken … That makes reclamation race-safe … if that owner were still\n> alive it would still hold this fcntl lock and we could not be here.\n\nDegrade to mkdir-only and `fcntl_held=True` becomes **a lie**. rebar would reclaim a write lock held\nby a **live** process. That is silent store corruption under concurrency — strictly worse than not\nsupporting Windows at all, because the failure is invisible instead of loud.\n\n## What a correct fix requires\n\n1. **A real alternative exclusive leg** on Windows — `msvcrt.locking(fd, LK_NBLCK, …)` or\n   `LockFileEx` via `ctypes`/`pywin32` — that provides the same *kernel-mediated, dies-with-the-\n   process* exclusivity `flock` provides. A userspace substitute that does not die with its holder\n   does NOT satisfy the precondition.\n2. **A reworked staleness proof.** The current proof leans on `flock` being kernel-mediated on the\n   inode and therefore shared across pid/mount namespaces on one kernel. The Windows leg's proof\n   must be stated explicitly in the docstring and must be its own argument, not a copy of the POSIX\n   one. If the Windows leg cannot support `fcntl_held=True`, the reclamation path must be narrowed\n   on Windows rather than the flag falsified.\n3. **A test that fails if the invariant is degraded** — the epic's AC5 and `f850`'s AC5, carried\n   forward unchanged. A change that makes `_acquire_mkdir` reachable without an exclusive leg held\n   must turn a test red.\n\n## This story GATES the rest of the epic\n\nUntil store writes work on Windows, the other classes cannot be measured: their tests fail during\nstore setup before reaching the semantics under test. Concretely, the bug's class list named\n\".EXE console-script invocation\" as an independent class, but the log shows those are\n`subprocess.CalledProcessError` on `…\\.venv\\Scripts\\rebar.EXE 'create' 'task'` whose captured\nstderr is this very `flock` `AttributeError` — downstream of S1, not separate. Expect the residual\nclass sizes to move once this lands.\n\n`S1 blocks` every other child of the epic.\n\n## Proving command\n\n```\nuv run --no-sync pytest tests/unit/test_store_write_lock.py tests/unit/test_lock_owner_staleness.py \\\n  tests/interfaces/store/test_mcp_write_lock_dead_stamp.py \\\n  tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/mutate/test_inbound_leaf_bodies.py \\\n  -p no:cacheprovider -q\n```\nrun on `windows-latest`, plus the unchanged POSIX run of the same selection on `ubuntu-latest` to\nprove no regression of the existing proof. (Adjust the first two paths to the actual lock test\nmodules present at implementation time; the third and fourth are named from the observed failure\ncensus — `test_mcp_write_lock_dead_stamp.py` is a collection ERROR today and\n`test_inbound_leaf_bodies.py` is the single largest failing file at 27.)\n\nWhole-class check afterwards:\n```\nuv run --no-sync pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -q -p no:cacheprovider 2>&1 \\\n  | grep -c \"has no attribute 'flock'\"   # must be 0\n```\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `fcntl` is never dereferenced when it is `None`; a Windows run reaches the same exclusivity\n   guarantee by a different documented mechanism.\n2. `_acquire_mkdir`'s `fcntl_held=True` argument is either still true under the Windows leg, with\n   the proof written out, or is not passed on Windows and reclamation is correspondingly narrowed.\n3. A test fails if the exclusivity precondition is degraded.\n4. The `flock` `AttributeError` count in a Windows sweep is 0.\n","id":"4cfc-2de2-747d-4388","parent_id":"55da-cee1-e556-4735","priority":1,"tags":["windows","portability","store","locking","blocking"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"S1: give _store/lock.py a real Windows exclusive-lock leg and rework the staleness proof it carries"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788571309227989965,"uuid":"79cab0aa-e058-4455-810d-c496744a55d6"}